    "lawCode": "Amd §§1202, 1205 & 1264, Pub Auth L",
    "lawSection": "Public Authorities Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S8328\n\nSPONSOR: HOYLMAN\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public authorities law, in relation to requiring the\nNew York City transit authority to operate its facilities and level of\nservice on a daily twenty-four hour basis unless a declared state of\nemergency is in effect, requiring the New York City transit authority or\nthe metropolitan transportation authority subway system to require that\nthe levels of service furnished upon the rapid transit facilities main-\ntained in the city of New York provide continuous service for twenty-\nfour hours a day when a declared state of emergency is not in effect,\nand requiring the metropolitan transportation authority to require the\nNew York City transit authority railroad facilities and levels of\nservice to be operated and maintained on a daily twenty-four hour basis\nunless a declared state of emergency is in effect\n\n \nPURPOSE OF BILL:\n\nThe purpose of this legislation is to require the New York City subway\nand bus systems to offer continuous 24/7 service when a declared state\nof emergency is not in effect.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1 of the bill amends Section 1202 of the Public Authorities Law,\nwhich sets forth the purposes of the New York City Transit Authority\n(NYCTA), to provide that the NYCTA's transit facilities shall be contin-\nuously operated on a daily twenty-four hour basis unless a declared\nstate of emergency is in effect.\n\nSection 2 of the bill amends Section 1205 of the Public Authorities Law,\nwhich relates to rates of fare and levels of service of the NYCTA's\nfacilities, to add a new subdivision 9 requiring the NYCTA or the Metro-\npolitan Transportation Authority (MTA) to require that the levels of\nservice furnished upon the NYCTA's rapid transit facilities in NYC shall\nprovide continuous daily service for 24 hours a day except when a\ndeclared state of emergency is in effect. Sub. 9 would also provide that\nin the event of a reduction in service during a declared state of emer-\ngency, there shall be no substantial or general change in the levels of\nservice furnished upon such facilities after the emergency has been\nlifted relative to the time before such emergency declaration, except\nupon the approval of a majority of the entire board of both the MTA and\nNYCTA.\n\nSection 3 of the bill amends Section 1264 of the Public Authorities Law,\nwhich sets forth the purposes of the MTA, to provide that the MTA's\nunified mass transportation policy shall include maintenance of contin-\nuous operation of the NYCTA's transit facilities and levels of service\non a daily twenty-four hour basis unless a declared state of emergency\nis in effect.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nThe COVID-19 pandemic has required New York State to take many\nunprecedented steps in order to keep New Yorkers safe and ensure that\nour state continues to maintain essential functions, such as closing\ndown schools, libraries, and most businesses, ordering people to wear\nmasks and practice \"social distancing,\" and asking people to stay in\ntheir homes whenever possible.\n\nAnother step which literally has no precedent is the decision, beginning\nin the early hours of May 6, 2020, to close the New York City subway\nsystem overnight from 1 am - 5am in order to thoroughly clean and disin-\nfect it. This marks the first time that the subway has intentionally\nshut down overnight since it opened 115 years ago.  The subway is the\nlifeblood of \"The City That Never Sleeps\" - a public utility that keeps\nNew York City moving at all hours of the day and night. It is an abso-\nlutely essential method of transportation for millions of New Yorkers,\nmany of whom have few alternative options for swift travel to places\nlike work, school, and health care facilities.\n\nThis pandemic has required New York's government to make many terribly\ndifficult choices. Public health experts tell us it is necessary to\ntemporarily close the subway overnight in order to protect public\nhealth, which continues to be the highest calling of all public offi-\ncials.\n\nHowever, once the worst effects of the COVID-19 pandemic are behind us\nand our State of Emergency is lifted, the MTA and NYC Transit Authority\nmust restore the 24/7 service New Yorkers have relied on for decades. We\ncannot let this virus leave behind a city that is less vibrant, less\nconnected or less equitable than it was before To accomplish this goal,\nthis bill would require the NYC subway and bus systems to offer contin-\nuous 24/7 service when a declared state of emergency is not in effect.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNone, new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
